+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

NeoMutt locks a fixed unveil(2) filesystem policy, then applies pledge(2)
before reading configuration. The policy allows the resolved home directory
read-only, a private per-run temporary directory, /var/mail when present,
existing ~/Mail, ~/mail, ~/.cache/neomutt and ~/.local/share/neomutt
directories, system and package configuration directories, terminal data,
DNS and password lookup files, and the base-system TLS certificate store.
Running as root is refused.

Paths outside those locations are unavailable. In particular, a -F
configuration file or source command outside the home and fixed system
configuration directories, mailboxes outside /var/mail and existing
writable mail directories under HOME, custom cache or certificate paths, and
attachments outside the allowed writable roots may fail. Other files under
HOME are readable but not writable through NeoMutt. Paths chosen after
startup are not added to the unveil policy. NeoMutt uses a private
temporary directory under HOME instead of exposing all of /tmp; it attempts
to remove that directory at exit.

The pledge policy omits exec. External programs cannot run, including
editors, pagers, mailcap handlers, sendmail, tunnels, filters, shell commands,
GnuPG helpers, and external SASL mechanisms. Forking remains permitted for
NeoMutt's process-control paths, but a helper's exec attempt violates pledge
and the helper is killed. GPGME operations needing a GnuPG process and SASL
mechanisms needing helpers are unavailable. The flavors remain buildable but
have these runtime limits.

The pledge call is made after unveil is locked and leaves execpromises
unset. No claim is made that external programs are confined by this policy.
Do not restore exec without reviewing the process and filesystem boundaries
together.
